window.__courseChapter("v1-09", {"v1-09-00-overview": {"title": "Overview: the big ideas", "summary": "The chapter's key concepts and how its sections fit together.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["Purpose", "concept map"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "Welcome to **Data Selection** (Vol I). This overview gives you the chapter's big ideas before the section-by-section lessons. Read the claims once, flip the flashcards, then take the warm-up quiz: it is fine to miss questions here, the lessons that follow fill the gaps."}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, Purpose", "md": "Training pays to acquire, label, score, move, and process examples even though their learning value differs. Large datasets contain redundant, noisy, or target-misaligned samples, while scarce regions may lack the cases a model needs, such as the rare conditions under which a deployed system is most likely to fail."}, {"type": "flow", "title": "The path through this chapter", "nodes": [{"label": "The Data Wall", "detail": "Lesson 1 covers this section."}, {"label": "Measuring Data Value", "detail": "Lesson 2 covers this section."}, {"label": "Static Pruning", "detail": "Lesson 3 covers this section."}, {"label": "Dynamic Selection", "detail": "Lesson 4 covers this section."}, {"label": "Synthetic Data Generation", "detail": "Lesson 5 covers this section."}, {"label": "Learning from Unlabeled Data", "detail": "Lesson 6 covers this section."}, {"label": "Selection Engineering", "detail": "Lesson 7 covers this section."}]}, {"type": "table", "headers": ["Concept", "The claim to remember"], "rows": [["**Selection inequality**", "A training-time claim for data selection holds only if selection time plus subset training time is less than full training time at matched quality and coverage, so scorer size and refresh rate, not the retained fraction, decide whether selection is a net saving."], ["**Measuring data value**", "Sample value is learning per unit of training compute (the information-compute ratio), learning-curve metrics show whether a reduction held quality, and a gain in data value becomes a throughput gain only when the run is compute-bound and the saved work is on the critical path."], ["**Compute-optimal frontier**", "Controlled sweeps fit a compute-optimal balance of about 20 tokens per parameter, so a run whose unique tokens per parameter sit well below it is data-starved and repeats its corpus, which is the regime where selection rather than more compute moves the run toward the frontier."], ["**Selection engineering on the data path**", "Selection removes accelerator work but adds host hashing, scoring passes, scattered reads, and shard coordination, so a retained subset saves reads only if materialized into its own shards, and data echoing recovers idle accelerator cycles when host preparation is the slow stage."], ["**Static pruning with coverage floors**", "Deduplication, quality pruning, and coreset selection shrink training work before training, but a score ranking must be constrained by per-class or per-slice coverage floors, which carry an explicit opportunity cost and can make a budget infeasible."], ["**Dynamic selection**", "Because sample value changes as the model learns, curriculum learning reorders data by difficulty and pacing, and active learning turns labeling into a query loop whose label saving must be measured against a budget-matched random baseline after pool-scoring cost."], ["**Synthetic data as a lever**", "When the pool lacks the needed cases, augmentation and generative synthesis replace curation at a per-sample cost against realism, the domain gap needs randomization, adaptation, and a real-data anchor, and recursive training risks model collapse."], ["**Learning from unlabeled data**", "Semi-supervised and self-supervised learning trade extra training compute for fewer task labels, valid only when the unlabeled pool matches the target distribution, and a foundation model pays off only when pretraining is amortized across enough downstream tasks."], ["**Data cost and amortized ROI**", "Total data cost is acquisition plus labeling plus storage plus processing, and a selection investment is judged by amortized return across all the runs it serves, so a pipeline that loses money on one run can pay across a campaign."]]}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "key", "title": "Formulas you will meet", "md": "$$\\text{ICR} = \\frac{\\Delta I}{\\Delta \\text{FLOPs}}$$\n\n$$\\text{ICR}(D) = \\frac{\\frac{d}{dD} I(D)}{\\frac{d}{dD} C(D)} \\approx \\frac{1/D}{O} = \\frac{1}{O \\cdot D}$$"}, {"type": "flashcards", "id": "v1-09-cards-concepts", "title": "Key concepts", "cards": [{"front": "Selection inequality", "back": "A training-time claim for data selection holds only if selection time plus subset training time is less than full training time at matched quality and coverage, so scorer size and refresh rate, not the retained fraction, decide whether selection is a net saving."}, {"front": "Measuring data value", "back": "Sample value is learning per unit of training compute (the information-compute ratio), learning-curve metrics show whether a reduction held quality, and a gain in data value becomes a throughput gain only when the run is compute-bound and the saved work is on the critical path."}, {"front": "Compute-optimal frontier", "back": "Controlled sweeps fit a compute-optimal balance of about 20 tokens per parameter, so a run whose unique tokens per parameter sit well below it is data-starved and repeats its corpus, which is the regime where selection rather than more compute moves the run toward the frontier."}, {"front": "Selection engineering on the data path", "back": "Selection removes accelerator work but adds host hashing, scoring passes, scattered reads, and shard coordination, so a retained subset saves reads only if materialized into its own shards, and data echoing recovers idle accelerator cycles when host preparation is the slow stage."}, {"front": "Static pruning with coverage floors", "back": "Deduplication, quality pruning, and coreset selection shrink training work before training, but a score ranking must be constrained by per-class or per-slice coverage floors, which carry an explicit opportunity cost and can make a budget infeasible."}, {"front": "Dynamic selection", "back": "Because sample value changes as the model learns, curriculum learning reorders data by difficulty and pacing, and active learning turns labeling into a query loop whose label saving must be measured against a budget-matched random baseline after pool-scoring cost."}, {"front": "Synthetic data as a lever", "back": "When the pool lacks the needed cases, augmentation and generative synthesis replace curation at a per-sample cost against realism, the domain gap needs randomization, adaptation, and a real-data anchor, and recursive training risks model collapse."}, {"front": "Learning from unlabeled data", "back": "Semi-supervised and self-supervised learning trade extra training compute for fewer task labels, valid only when the unlabeled pool matches the target distribution, and a foundation model pays off only when pretraining is amortized across enough downstream tasks."}, {"front": "Data cost and amortized ROI", "back": "Total data cost is acquisition plus labeling plus storage plus processing, and a selection investment is judged by amortized return across all the runs it serves, so a pipeline that loses money on one run can pay across a campaign."}]}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-quiz-warmup", "title": "Warm-up: match the big ideas", "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Selection inequality**?", "options": ["A training-time claim for data selection holds only if selection time plus subset training time is less than full training time at matched quality and coverage, so scorer size and refresh rate, not the retained fraction, decide whether selection is a net saving.", "Controlled sweeps fit a compute-optimal balance of about 20 tokens per parameter, so a run whose unique tokens per parameter sit well below it is data-starved and repeats its corpus, which is the regime where selection rather than more compute moves the run toward the frontier.", "Sample value is learning per unit of training compute (the information-compute ratio), learning-curve metrics show whether a reduction held quality, and a gain in data value becomes a throughput gain only when the run is compute-bound and the saved work is on the critical path.", "When the pool lacks the needed cases, augmentation and generative synthesis replace curation at a per-sample cost against realism, the domain gap needs randomization, adaptation, and a real-data anchor, and recursive training risks model collapse."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "**Selection inequality:** A training-time claim for data selection holds only if selection time plus subset training time is less than full training time at matched quality and coverage, so scorer size and refresh rate, not the retained fraction, decide whether selection is a net saving.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Selection inequality itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Measuring data value**?", "options": ["A training-time claim for data selection holds only if selection time plus subset training time is less than full training time at matched quality and coverage, so scorer size and refresh rate, not the retained fraction, decide whether selection is a net saving.", "Deduplication, quality pruning, and coreset selection shrink training work before training, but a score ranking must be constrained by per-class or per-slice coverage floors, which carry an explicit opportunity cost and can make a budget infeasible.", "Selection removes accelerator work but adds host hashing, scoring passes, scattered reads, and shard coordination, so a retained subset saves reads only if materialized into its own shards, and data echoing recovers idle accelerator cycles when host preparation is the slow stage.", "Sample value is learning per unit of training compute (the information-compute ratio), learning-curve metrics show whether a reduction held quality, and a gain in data value becomes a throughput gain only when the run is compute-bound and the saved work is on the critical path."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Measuring data value:** Sample value is learning per unit of training compute (the information-compute ratio), learning-curve metrics show whether a reduction held quality, and a gain in data value becomes a throughput gain only when the run is compute-bound and the saved work is on the critical path.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Measuring data value itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Compute-optimal frontier**?", "options": ["Sample value is learning per unit of training compute (the information-compute ratio), learning-curve metrics show whether a reduction held quality, and a gain in data value becomes a throughput gain only when the run is compute-bound and the saved work is on the critical path.", "Controlled sweeps fit a compute-optimal balance of about 20 tokens per parameter, so a run whose unique tokens per parameter sit well below it is data-starved and repeats its corpus, which is the regime where selection rather than more compute moves the run toward the frontier.", "Semi-supervised and self-supervised learning trade extra training compute for fewer task labels, valid only when the unlabeled pool matches the target distribution, and a foundation model pays off only when pretraining is amortized across enough downstream tasks.", "When the pool lacks the needed cases, augmentation and generative synthesis replace curation at a per-sample cost against realism, the domain gap needs randomization, adaptation, and a real-data anchor, and recursive training risks model collapse."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Compute-optimal frontier:** Controlled sweeps fit a compute-optimal balance of about 20 tokens per parameter, so a run whose unique tokens per parameter sit well below it is data-starved and repeats its corpus, which is the regime where selection rather than more compute moves the run toward the frontier.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Compute-optimal frontier itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Selection engineering on the data path**?", "options": ["Because sample value changes as the model learns, curriculum learning reorders data by difficulty and pacing, and active learning turns labeling into a query loop whose label saving must be measured against a budget-matched random baseline after pool-scoring cost.", "Semi-supervised and self-supervised learning trade extra training compute for fewer task labels, valid only when the unlabeled pool matches the target distribution, and a foundation model pays off only when pretraining is amortized across enough downstream tasks.", "Selection removes accelerator work but adds host hashing, scoring passes, scattered reads, and shard coordination, so a retained subset saves reads only if materialized into its own shards, and data echoing recovers idle accelerator cycles when host preparation is the slow stage.", "A training-time claim for data selection holds only if selection time plus subset training time is less than full training time at matched quality and coverage, so scorer size and refresh rate, not the retained fraction, decide whether selection is a net saving."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Selection engineering on the data path:** Selection removes accelerator work but adds host hashing, scoring passes, scattered reads, and shard coordination, so a retained subset saves reads only if materialized into its own shards, and data echoing recovers idle accelerator cycles when host preparation is the slow stage.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Selection engineering on the data path itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Static pruning with coverage floors**?", "options": ["Total data cost is acquisition plus labeling plus storage plus processing, and a selection investment is judged by amortized return across all the runs it serves, so a pipeline that loses money on one run can pay across a campaign.", "Selection removes accelerator work but adds host hashing, scoring passes, scattered reads, and shard coordination, so a retained subset saves reads only if materialized into its own shards, and data echoing recovers idle accelerator cycles when host preparation is the slow stage.", "A training-time claim for data selection holds only if selection time plus subset training time is less than full training time at matched quality and coverage, so scorer size and refresh rate, not the retained fraction, decide whether selection is a net saving.", "Deduplication, quality pruning, and coreset selection shrink training work before training, but a score ranking must be constrained by per-class or per-slice coverage floors, which carry an explicit opportunity cost and can make a budget infeasible."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Static pruning with coverage floors:** Deduplication, quality pruning, and coreset selection shrink training work before training, but a score ranking must be constrained by per-class or per-slice coverage floors, which carry an explicit opportunity cost and can make a budget infeasible.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Static pruning with coverage floors itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["Selection inequality: A training-time claim for data selection holds only if selection time plus subset training time is less than full training time at matched quality and coverage, so scorer size and refresh rate, not the retained fraction, decide whether selection is a net saving.", "Measuring data value: Sample value is learning per unit of training compute (the information-compute ratio), learning-curve metrics show whether a reduction held quality, and a gain in data value becomes a throughput gain only when the run is compute-bound and the saved work is on the critical path.", "Compute-optimal frontier: Controlled sweeps fit a compute-optimal balance of about 20 tokens per parameter, so a run whose unique tokens per parameter sit well below it is data-starved and repeats its corpus, which is the regime where selection rather than more compute moves the run toward the frontier.", "Selection engineering on the data path: Selection removes accelerator work but adds host hashing, scoring passes, scattered reads, and shard coordination, so a retained subset saves reads only if materialized into its own shards, and data echoing recovers idle accelerator cycles when host preparation is the slow stage."]}], "terms": [], "id": "v1-09-00-overview", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 10}, "v1-09-01-the-data-wall": {"title": "The Data Wall", "summary": "Analyze the systems implications of the compute-to-data scaling asymmetry and diagnose the data wall regime.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["The Data Wall"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **The Data Wall** in *Data Selection* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze the systems implications of the compute-to-data scaling asymmetry and diagnose the data wall regime\n- Evaluate the misconception that information value scales linearly with raw corpus volume\n- Identify the chapter's conceptual ordering of the three main data-selection intervention points"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, § The Data Wall", "md": "Every example a training run processes costs the same forward pass, backward pass, and optimizer update, whether it teaches the model something new or repeats what the model already knows. A duplicated image, a mislabeled record, and a rare boundary case cost the same arithmetic while contributing very different learning signals. @Sec-model-training made each of those steps as cheap as one machine allows and accounted for a whole run as roughly \\$6ParametersDatasetSize floating-point operations for Parameters parameters and DatasetSize tokens."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: Scaling asymmetry between compute and data; The data wall and diminishing returns from raw corpus growth; The three selection levers and when they act."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-01-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In an ML infrastructure scaling scenario where available GPU compute grows by approximately \\$10\\times$ every 3 years while high-quality web data grows by only \\$2\\times$ every 5 years, what primary systems regime emerges, and what is the appropriate systems response?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Compute throughput growing faster than accessible high-quality human data creates a compute-to-data imbalance known as the data wall. In this regime, additional raw compute yields diminishing returns on uncurated corpora, making data selection essential to maximize the Information-Compute Ratio (ICR). Shifting model parallelism addresses parameter memory limits rather than data scarcity, scaling disk bandwidth solves I/O stalls rather than token information quality, and eliminating synthetic generation ignores a key strategy for expanding scarce data pools.", "type": "single", "options": ["A compute-rich, data-constrained data wall regime where intelligent data selection and curation must maximize the learning signal extracted per token", "A memory bandwidth-bound regime where model parallel sharding must replace data parallelism across all training clusters", "An I/O ingestion bottleneck where disk read bandwidth must be quadrupled to keep GPUs saturated", "A compute-starved regime where synthetic data generation should be eliminated to avoid wasting accelerator cycles"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the systems implications of the compute-to-data scaling asymmetry and diagnose the data wall regime."]}, {"q": "Doubling the amount of raw, deduplicated web data will always double the total information learned by the model.", "explanation": "False. The section argues for diminishing returns, not a universal doubling law: additional data can add much less marginal signal than its extra compute cost. That is why teams must evaluate learning curves and target quality empirically instead of assuming linear gains from corpus size.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the misconception that information value scales linearly with raw corpus volume."]}, {"q": "Order the following stages as they are presented in this chapter's conceptual map of the data-selection pipeline:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (2) Static Pruning, (1) Dynamic Selection, (3) Synthetic Data Generation. This is the chapter's presentation order: prune low-value data before target training, adapt sample usage during training, then create missing coverage when needed. The sequence is a conceptual map rather than a mandatory runtime chronology, because synthetic data can also be created earlier and then pruned or selected before final training.", "type": "order", "items": ["Static Pruning", "Dynamic Selection", "Synthetic Data Generation."], "hints": ["Think about: identify the chapter's conceptual ordering of the three main data-selection intervention points."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze the systems implications of the compute-to-data scaling asymmetry and diagnose the data wall regime.", "You can now evaluate the misconception that information value scales linearly with raw corpus volume.", "You can now identify the chapter's conceptual ordering of the three main data-selection intervention points."]}], "id": "v1-09-01-the-data-wall", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-09-02-measuring-data-value": {"title": "Measuring Data Value", "summary": "Calculate the scaling behavior of the Information-Compute Ratio under the section's illustrative logarithmic-information model.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["Measuring Data Value", "The information-compute ratio", "Diminishing returns and the ICR frontier", "The compute-optimal frontier", "Core metrics", "Validating a reduced dataset", "The selection inequality"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Measuring Data Value** in *Data Selection* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate the scaling behavior of the Information-Compute Ratio under the section's illustrative logarithmic-information model\n- Compare data correctness with data value in the Information-Compute Ratio framework\n- Interpret a controlled compute-optimal-frontier sweep without over-inferring a unique bottleneck from a single operating point\n- Match the chapter's core metrics to the measurement goals they are designed to answer\n- Justify AULC as a data-efficiency metric by relating it to performance across dataset sizes rather than a single endpoint"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, § Measuring Data Value", "md": "A ResNet-50 trained on an aggressively filtered ImageNet subset can hold its aggregate accuracy while failing on rare classes that the filter discarded as low-density noise. A GPT-2 run on a filtered corpus can cost less per epoch while needing more epochs to reach the same loss. A measurement framework is therefore needed to show what a reduction actually saves."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- The information-compute ratio\n- Diminishing returns and the ICR frontier\n- The compute-optimal frontier\n- Core metrics\n- Validating a reduced dataset\n- The selection inequality\n\nPay attention to: Information-compute ratio and its marginal decay; Data correctness versus data value; Compute-optimal diagnosis; PPD, AULC, and DCR."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why a dataset where \\$100\\% of the sample labels are verifiably correct can still exhibit a very low Information-Compute Ratio (ICR).", "md": "Label correctness measures ground-truth accuracy, whereas ICR measures evaluation gain per unit of training compute over a stated interval. A dataset of completely correct examples will have near-zero ICR if the samples are redundant, easily classified by the current model state, or already mastered, because processing them consumes forward and backward FLOPs without producing a corresponding evaluation gain; parameter updates alone do not establish learning value."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-02-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Under the illustrative analytical model where dataset information content scales as $\\mathcal{I}(D) \\propto \\log D$ and training compute scales linearly as $O = O_{\\text{sample}} \\cdot D$, how does the marginal Information-Compute Ratio $\\mathrm{ICR}(D)$ scale with dataset size $D$?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Under the chapter's illustrative model, $d\\mathcal{I}/dD \\propto 1/D$ and $dO/dD = O_{\\text{sample}}$, so their ratio is $\\mathrm{ICR}(D) \\approx 1/(O_{\\text{sample}}D)$. This captures diminishing marginal learning signal under the stated log-information and linear-compute assumptions. A constant or growing answer ignores the derivative of $\\log D$, while the exponential-decay choice introduces a memory-capacity mechanism that is not part of this model.", "type": "single", "options": ["It remains constant at $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ because additional compute scales proportionally with dataset size", "It decays as $\\mathcal{O}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{O_{\\text{sample}}D}\\right)$, so under this illustrative model additional data eventually adds compute faster than marginal information", "It grows logarithmically as $\\mathcal{O}(\\log D / O_{\\text{sample}})$ due to power-law parameter scaling", "It decays exponentially as $\\mathcal{O}(\\exp(-D))$ once the training corpus exceeds accelerator memory capacity"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the scaling behavior of the Information-Compute Ratio under the section's illustrative logarithmic-information model."]}, {"q": "An ML systems diagnostic plot maps normalized training compute on the horizontal axis against model performance on the vertical axis. A 10M-parameter run sits well below a fitted frontier under a fixed evaluation protocol and fixed total budget. A controlled sweep then shows that reallocating the same budget toward a larger model improves performance. What is the most defensible diagnosis of the original operating point?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Under the chapter's measurement framework, a point below a fitted frontier does not identify a unique cause by itself. What the controlled sweep supports is that the original allocation of budget across model size and data was suboptimal for the tested regime. Calling it purely data-starved or purely capacity-limited would overclaim from one observation, and the other choices describe different bottlenecks entirely.", "type": "single", "options": ["Data-starved regime", "I/O bandwidth-saturated regime", "The model/data allocation was suboptimal under the tested budget", "Over-echoing regime"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: interpret a controlled compute-optimal-frontier sweep without over-inferring a unique bottleneck from a single operating point."]}, {"q": "A team wants one metric for how much smaller a selected dataset is at a target accuracy, and another for how much learning is gained per sample over a measured interval. Which pair of chapter metrics matches that need?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Data Compression Ratio (DCR) asks how much the dataset can be reduced while still reaching a target accuracy, and Performance-Per-Data (PPD) measures accuracy gain per sample over a stated interval. The other choices introduce names the chapter does not use. AULC is another valid core metric, but it summarizes performance across dataset sizes rather than serving as the per-sample interval metric in the question.", "type": "single", "options": ["Data Curation Rate and Accuracy Reduction Ratio", "Data Compression Ratio and Performance-Per-Data", "Dynamic Checkpoint Rate and Active Retention Rate", "Data Convergence Ratio and Amortized Risk Ratio"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: match the chapter's core metrics to the measurement goals they are designed to answer."]}, {"q": "A training team reduces a dataset from \\$1{,}000{,}000$ to \\$100{,}000$ images. Full-data training takes 10 hours, subset training takes 1 hour, and scoring the full pool with the production model takes 12 hours. Which conclusion and next step best fit the selection inequality?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The chapter's time-saving test is T_selection+T_train(D_subset)<T_train(D_total). Here, \\$12+1=13 hours, which is worse than the 10-hour baseline. Proxy models or cached embeddings are sensible candidate fixes, but they only restore training-time saving if measured scoring time falls below the 9-hour savings window and the selected subset still preserves target quality. A large reduction ratio alone does not prove success, and slower storage would move in the wrong direction.", "type": "single", "options": ["Yes, the inequality is satisfied because a \\$90\\% dataset reduction guarantees positive ROI", "Yes, because the 9 hours saved in training outweigh the scoring cost; the team only needs more GPUs", "No, because \\$12+1=13 hours exceeds the 10-hour baseline; a plausible fix is proxy scoring or cached embeddings, but the team must verify that scoring drops below 9 hours while target quality holds", "No, because coreset training always increases memory-bandwidth consumption; the team should switch from NVMe SSDs to HDDs"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: apply the selection inequality to diagnose when scoring overhead erases subset-training savings and identify what must be re-measured after a proposed fix."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Why is Area Under the Learning Curve (AULC) a more informative metric than single-point final validation accuracy when comparing data selection methods across dataset sizes?", "md": "AULC integrates performance over dataset size $n$, so it rewards methods that achieve stronger accuracy earlier in the data-allocation range rather than judging them only at one endpoint. Under the same evaluation protocol and integration limits, a higher AULC means better aggregate performance over that same dataset-size range. The practical caveat is that this does not automatically imply the method is better at every dataset size, nor does it guarantee lower total FLOPs if per-sample cost differs across methods."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate the scaling behavior of the Information-Compute Ratio under the section's illustrative logarithmic-information model.", "You can now compare data correctness with data value in the Information-Compute Ratio framework.", "You can now interpret a controlled compute-optimal-frontier sweep without over-inferring a unique bottleneck from a single operating point.", "You can now match the chapter's core metrics to the measurement goals they are designed to answer."]}], "id": "v1-09-02-measuring-data-value", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-09-03-static-pruning": {"title": "Static Pruning", "summary": "Compare proxy-based EL2N scoring with other coreset methods in terms of cost, signal, and validation needs.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["Static Pruning", "The case for smaller datasets", "Data deduplication", "Data pruning by quality", "Coreset selection algorithms"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Static Pruning** in *Data Selection* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Compare proxy-based EL2N scoring with other coreset methods in terms of cost, signal, and validation needs\n- Analyze the computational scaling benefits of MinHash and Locality-Sensitive Hashing for web-scale deduplication\n- Evaluate the systems and statistical trade-offs of aggressive near-duplicate filtering\n- Identify EL2N from its mathematical definition and explain why proxy-based use still requires validation"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, § Static Pruning", "md": "Web-scale scraping routinely gathers boilerplate text, duplicate images, and malformed records, and each one costs GPT-2 six FLOPs per parameter for every token, or ResNet-50 a full forward and backward pass, before a single useful gradient is calculated. Static pruning removes these low-value records upstream of the training loop. By dropping redundant and corrupt records before target-model training begins, static pruning shrinks the operation count (Operations) without changing model architectures or execution kernels."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- The case for smaller datasets\n- Data deduplication\n- Data pruning by quality\n- Coreset selection algorithms\n\nPay attention to: Proxy-based coreset scoring; MinHash and LSH deduplication; Validating near-duplicate removal; EL2N."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-03-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A team wants to score a dataset cheaply before training a large production model. They plan to train a small proxy for a few epochs and rank examples by prediction error, knowing that confidently wrong predictions can still score highly. Which method best matches that plan and its trade-off?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. EL2N ranks samples by early prediction error $\\lVert \\hat{\\mathbf{y}}-\\mathbf{y} \\rVert_2$, so a cheap proxy can score examples without a full target-model run. The section also warns that high EL2N can include noisy samples and that transfer from proxy to target is useful but not guaranteed. Geometry-based $k$-Center is not label-aware boundary scoring, Forgetting Events is much more expensive, and Herding still depends on feature representations.", "type": "single", "options": ["$k$-Center clustering on raw pixel inputs, because it guarantees zero-cost label-aware boundary identification", "Forgetting Events scoring on the full production model, because it requires no proxy architecture and computes in $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ time", "EL2N (Error L2-Norm) scoring with a briefly trained proxy model, accepting that high error can reflect hard examples or noise", "Herding on Gaussian-distributed features, because it completely avoids computing feature representations"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: compare proxy-based EL2N scoring with other coreset methods in terms of cost, signal, and validation needs."]}, {"q": "Why is Locality-Sensitive Hashing (LSH) with MinHash preferred over exhaustive pairwise Jaccard similarity comparison for large-scale text deduplication?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Comparing all pairs in a dataset of $D$ documents requires $\\mathcal{O}(D^2)$ comparisons, which is computationally intractable for web-scale datasets ($D \\ge 10^7$). MinHash compresses documents into fixed-size sketches where collision probability equals Jaccard similarity, and LSH bands these sketches into hash buckets so only candidate pairs colliding in a bucket are compared. MinHash LSH does not detect cross-lingual semantic paraphrases, still requires shingling/tokenization, and pairwise Jaccard comparison is an exact set operation rather than a deep learning method.", "type": "single", "options": ["MinHash LSH guarantees \\$100\\% precision in detecting semantic paraphrases across different natural languages", "MinHash eliminates the need to tokenize or shingle input documents before hashing", "Exhaustive pairwise Jaccard comparison requires training a deep neural network, whereas LSH is purely rule-based", "Exhaustive pairwise comparison requires $\\mathcal{O}(D^2)$ document comparisons, whereas MinHash LSH hashes compact signatures into sublinear candidate collision buckets"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the computational scaling benefits of MinHash and Locality-Sensitive Hashing for web-scale deduplication."]}, {"q": "Removing near-duplicate documents with MinHash LSH always improves model accuracy because duplicate data has zero statistical value in all training regimes.", "explanation": "False. While deduplication reduces redundant computation and memorization risk, in some domains duplicate frequency reflects natural empirical data distributions or intentional weighting. Aggressive deduplication with miscalibrated thresholds can prune legitimate variations or distort class priors, necessitating empirical validation on downstream accuracy.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the systems and statistical trade-offs of aggressive near-duplicate filtering."]}, {"q": "The training-dynamics coreset metric that measures the Euclidean norm of the difference between predicted class probabilities and the one-hot target vector early in training is known as ____.", "explanation": "EL2N. It measures $\\lVert p(x)-y \\rVert_2$ early in training as an error-based difficulty score. The chapter notes that proxy-model rankings can transfer usefully to larger targets, but that transfer must be validated rather than assumed universal.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["EL2N"], "hints": ["Think about: identify EL2N from its mathematical definition and explain why proxy-based use still requires validation."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now compare proxy-based EL2N scoring with other coreset methods in terms of cost, signal, and validation needs.", "You can now analyze the computational scaling benefits of MinHash and Locality-Sensitive Hashing for web-scale deduplication.", "You can now evaluate the systems and statistical trade-offs of aggressive near-duplicate filtering.", "You can now identify EL2N from its mathematical definition and explain why proxy-based use still requires validation."]}], "id": "v1-09-03-static-pruning", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-09-04-dynamic-selection": {"title": "Dynamic Selection", "summary": "Analyze how curriculum pacing can stabilize early learning by controlling when harder examples appear.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["Dynamic Selection", "Curriculum learning", "Active learning"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Dynamic Selection** in *Data Selection* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze how curriculum pacing can stabilize early learning by controlling when harder examples appear\n- Compare active learning query strategies to mitigate sampling bias and redundancy\n- Design the closed-loop workflow of an iterative active learning system"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, § Dynamic Selection", "md": "Early in a ResNet-50 run over ImageNet, clean, centered images of common classes provide stable gradients that establish general feature representations, but as parameters converge their gradient norms decay toward zero. Executing forward and backward passes on these mastered samples continues to consume accelerator execution cycles and high-bandwidth memory (HBM) transfers while producing negligible parameter updates, driving their effective ICR toward zero. Dynamic selection mitigates this waste by adapting batch composition to the model's current state."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Curriculum learning\n- Active learning\n\nPay attention to: Curriculum pacing; Active learning query strategies; The active learning loop."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-04-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In curriculum learning, an engineer implements an easy-to-hard pacing schedule that controls the fraction of the sorted training pool available at each stage. What is the primary objective of this pacing strategy?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The chapter presents curriculum learning as a way to shape the optimization trajectory: easier examples can provide steadier early gradients, while harder examples are introduced later as the model matures. That is not a guarantee against divergence on every task, and 'easy' depends on the scoring rule, but it is the pacing mechanism this section teaches. The other choices confuse curriculum with memory-layout tricks or active-learning workflows.", "type": "single", "options": ["To guide early optimization with more stable examples before gradually introducing harder ones", "To eliminate the need for backward passes during the first half of training", "To maximize GPU memory bandwidth utilization by sorting tensors strictly by length in bytes", "To replace human labelers with an automated oracle during the late stages of training"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze how curriculum pacing can stabilize early learning by controlling when harder examples appear."]}, {"q": "An active learning pipeline chooses unlabeled examples for costly radiologist annotation. The team notices that simple uncertainty sampling repeatedly selects images from a single ambiguous artifact class, starving other disease categories. Which query strategy should they adopt to resolve this pathology?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Pure uncertainty sampling often suffers from sampling bias by selecting clustered points near a single ambiguous boundary region. Diversity sampling (or hybrid strategies like BADGE that incorporate gradient embeddings) ensures that selected queries span diverse clusters across the feature space while still targeting model uncertainty. Least-confidence sampling exacerbates the clustering issue, random undersampling discards informative candidates arbitrarily, and unthresholded pseudo-labeling introduces catastrophic label noise.", "type": "single", "options": ["Least-confidence sampling, because it strictly selects the lowest top-1 probability prediction", "Diversity sampling or hybrid uncertainty-diversity sampling (such as BADGE), which balances uncertainty near decision boundaries with feature-space coverage", "Random undersampling of the entire unlabeled pool to reduce dataset size before scoring", "Uniform zero-shot pseudo-labeling without confidence thresholds"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: compare active learning query strategies to mitigate sampling bias and redundancy."]}, {"q": "Order the steps in an active learning closed-loop iteration:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (3) Score unlabeled pool using current model, (1) Select top query samples via query strategy, (2) Acquire expert annotations from the oracle, (5) Add newly labeled samples to the training set, (4) Retrain or update model on expanded dataset. The cycle begins with proxy or model scoring over the candidate pool, selecting candidate queries, querying the human oracle, incorporating verified labels into the dataset, and updating the model.", "type": "order", "items": ["Score unlabeled pool using current model", "Select top query samples via query strategy", "Acquire expert annotations from the oracle", "Add newly labeled samples to the training set.", "Retrain or update model on expanded dataset"], "hints": ["Think about: design the closed-loop workflow of an iterative active learning system."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze how curriculum pacing can stabilize early learning by controlling when harder examples appear.", "You can now compare active learning query strategies to mitigate sampling bias and redundancy.", "You can now design the closed-loop workflow of an iterative active learning system."]}], "id": "v1-09-04-dynamic-selection", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-09-05-synthetic-data-generation": {"title": "Synthetic Data Generation", "summary": "Analyze how recursive synthetic training can degrade tail coverage and diversity over generations.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["Synthetic Data Generation", "Data augmentation", "Generative synthesis", "Bridging the domain gap", "Knowledge distillation"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Synthetic Data Generation** in *Data Selection* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze how recursive synthetic training can degrade tail coverage and diversity over generations\n- Analyze knowledge distillation as a data-synthesis lever by locating its storage and input-pipeline cost\n- Analyze domain gap challenges and justify synthetic-to-real mixing ratios\n- Evaluate the misconception that advanced generative models eliminate domain shift"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, § Synthetic Data Generation", "md": "Real-world data collection encounters physical and legal boundaries: autonomous fleets cannot safely crash vehicles to acquire catastrophic collision trajectories, healthcare systems cannot transmit protected patient telemetry across institutional firewalls, and edge products must handle acoustic environments before any deployed fleet exists to record them. When the source corpus contains few or none of the cases deployment will face, neither static pruning nor dynamic selection can extract signal that is not there."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Data augmentation\n- Generative synthesis\n- Bridging the domain gap\n- Knowledge distillation\n\nPay attention to: Model collapse; Offline distillation targets as a data cost; Synthetic-real mixing and the domain gap; Limits of generated data."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why is training exclusively on \\$100\\% synthetic data from a simulation engine often suboptimal for real-world deployment, and how does synthetic-to-real data mixing bridge this gap?", "md": "Simulation engines have an inherent domain gap ($\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{synth}} \\ne \\mathcal{D}_{\\text{real}}$) caused by simplified physics, missing sensor artifacts, and unmodeled environmental noise. Mixing synthetic data with a curated set of real-world examples anchors the model to real feature distributions while using synthetic samples to expand volume, cover edge cases, and balance rare classes."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-05-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "What causes the phenomenon of 'model collapse' when generative models are trained recursively on synthetic data generated by earlier model iterations?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The section's model-collapse mechanism is recursive tail erosion: generated data underrepresents rare but important patterns, and retraining on later synthetic generations compounds those omissions and errors. High-probability modes dominating the sample stream is part of that story, but it does not prove universal collapse under every sampling regime. The other choices are execution problems, not the chapter's statistical mechanism.", "type": "single", "options": ["GPU memory fragmentation caused by variable-length synthetic sequences during distributed training", "Overfitting to floating-point rounding errors during fp16 mixed-precision matrix multiplication", "A failure of the I/O storage subsystem to deliver synthetic batches at line rate", "Systematic underrepresentation and erosion of tail patterns across generations, so recursive retraining compounds missing or distorted rare cases"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: analyze how recursive synthetic training can degrade tail coverage and diversity over generations."]}, {"q": "A team distills a ResNet-50 student on ImageNet and chooses offline distillation, precomputing the teacher's FP32 logits for every training image instead of keeping the teacher in accelerator memory. Which cost does this choice move onto the data path?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Offline distillation stores one logit per class for every sample, so the per-sample label grows from a single integer index to four bytes per class, an expansion of more than three orders of magnitude for ImageNet's thousand classes, and every epoch reads it back through the input pipeline. Teacher residency and the reduced student batch size are the costs of online distillation, which this choice avoids. Soft targets do not force a lower learning rate, and the teacher pass runs once during precomputation rather than once per epoch.", "type": "single", "options": ["Every epoch must read per-class logit vectors in place of single integer labels, multiplying the label bytes the storage tier and input pipeline must deliver", "Every training step must run a teacher forward pass beside the student, restricting the student's per-accelerator batch size", "The student must be trained with a lower learning rate, because soft targets produce larger gradients than one-hot labels", "The teacher must be retrained once per student epoch, so preparation cost grows linearly with the number of epochs"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze knowledge distillation as a data-synthesis lever by locating its storage and input-pipeline cost."]}, {"q": "Using a high-capacity diffusion model or state-of-the-art LLM to generate synthetic training data guarantees that the resulting training set will be completely free of distribution shift relative to the real deployment environment.", "explanation": "False. Generator quality does not eliminate domain gap. Synthetic generators can amplify subtle distribution shifts, introduce generator-specific artifacts, and omit edge cases present in real-world deployment environments, requiring domain validation against real ground truth.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the misconception that advanced generative models eliminate domain shift."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze how recursive synthetic training can degrade tail coverage and diversity over generations.", "You can now analyze knowledge distillation as a data-synthesis lever by locating its storage and input-pipeline cost.", "You can now analyze domain gap challenges and justify synthetic-to-real mixing ratios.", "You can now evaluate the misconception that advanced generative models eliminate domain shift."]}], "id": "v1-09-05-synthetic-data-generation", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-09-06-learning-from-unlabeled-data": {"title": "Learning from Unlabeled Data", "summary": "Explain confirmation bias in pseudo-labeling and evaluate what confidence thresholding can and cannot fix.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["Learning from Unlabeled Data", "Semi-supervised learning", "Self-supervised learning", "Foundation-model amortization"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Learning from Unlabeled Data** in *Data Selection* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain confirmation bias in pseudo-labeling and evaluate what confidence thresholding can and cannot fix\n- Evaluate the task-dependent assumption behind consistency regularization\n- Calculate the break-even threshold for amortizing self-supervised pretraining across downstream tasks\n- Compare contrastive self-supervised learning architectures and their memory-compute scaling trade-offs\n- Analyze why pretraining-data defects in a shared foundation model can affect many downstream adaptations"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, § Learning from Unlabeled Data", "md": "A hospital archive holding tens of thousands of unannotated chest X-rays or a language-modeling team holding terabytes of raw web text already has its inputs on disk; the bottleneck is that active learning still pays an expert for every example it selects and synthesis still pays for every example it creates. Learning from unlabeled data attacks that label bottleneck from the other side. Semi-supervised learning lets a model trained on a small labeled seed supply provisional labels for the rest of the pool."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Semi-supervised learning\n- Self-supervised learning\n- Foundation-model amortization\n\nPay attention to: Pseudo-labeling and confirmation bias; Consistency regularization; Foundation-model amortization; Contrastive learning memory cost."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain the mechanism of confirmation bias in semi-supervised pseudo-labeling, and specify how confidence thresholding mitigates it.", "md": "Confirmation bias arises when the model trains on its own wrong pseudo-labels and reinforces those mistakes in later rounds. A high confidence threshold filters out low-confidence predictions, but confidently wrong pseudo-labels can still survive, so thresholding is only a partial defense. The practical check is to compare the model's confidence against held-out labeled validation evidence before trusting the expanded training set."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-06-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Consistency regularization methods such as FixMatch assume that label-preserving perturbations of an input should not change the model's predicted class distribution much.", "explanation": "True. The method relies on a task-valid smoothness assumption: if the augmentation preserves the label, predictions across the two views should stay consistent. The key caveat is that closeness in raw input space alone is not enough—rotations, crops, or other perturbations can change the true label on some tasks.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": true, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the task-dependent assumption behind consistency regularization."]}, {"q": "In the economics of foundation models, an organization invests $C_{\\text{pretrain}} = 10{,}000$ GPU-hours in self-supervised pretraining. Each downstream task fine-tuning costs $C_{\\text{finetune}} = 50$ GPU-hours. Training each task from scratch would cost $C_{\\text{scratch}} = 1{,}000$ GPU-hours. What is the minimum number of downstream tasks $N^*$ required to break even on the pretraining investment?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The break-even condition is $C_{\\text{pretrain}} + N \\cdot C_{\\text{finetune}} \\le N \\cdot C_{\\text{scratch}}$, which gives \\$10{,}000 + 50N \\le 1000N$, or \\$950N \\ge 10{,}000$. Solving for $N$ yields $N \\ge 10{,}000 / 950 \\approx 10.53$. Thus, at least 11 downstream tasks are required for the foundation model approach to use fewer total GPU-hours under these assumptions. This is a compute-only crossover at matched target quality; a dollar comparison must also price labels, accelerator hours, and other differing costs. At 10 tasks, scratch training costs \\$10{,}000$ hours while the foundation model costs \\$10{,}500$ hours. At 11 tasks, scratch training costs \\$11{,}000$ hours while the foundation model costs \\$10{,}550$ hours.", "type": "single", "options": ["$N^* = 5$ downstream tasks", "$N^* = 8$ downstream tasks", "N^* = 11 downstream tasks (\\$10,000 + 11 × 50 = 10,550 < 11 × 1,000 = 11,000)", "$N^* = 50$ downstream tasks"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the break-even threshold for amortizing self-supervised pretraining across downstream tasks."]}, {"q": "How does the MoCo (Momentum Contrast) framework reduce the hardware and memory constraints of contrastive self-supervised learning compared to naive SimCLR?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Contrastive learning requires large sets of negative examples for effective representation learning. While SimCLR scales the mini-batch size (batches of up to 8,192 examples, paid for in activation memory and all-gather traffic), MoCo decouples the dictionary size from the mini-batch size by maintaining a memory queue of negative keys updated with a momentum-averaged teacher encoder. MoCo still uses neural backbones and backpropagation, operates in an unsupervised manner without human labels, and computes cosine similarities in latent space rather than raw byte Jaccard comparisons.", "type": "single", "options": ["By replacing convolutional backbones with rule-based lookup tables to avoid backpropagation", "By requiring fully supervised class labels to filter out false negative pairs", "By using a dynamic queue of negative keys and a slowly updating momentum encoder, decoupling negative dictionary size from mini-batch size", "By computing exact pairwise Jaccard similarities on raw byte sequences rather than latent embeddings"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: compare contrastive self-supervised learning architectures and their memory-compute scaling trade-offs."]}, {"q": "Because self-supervised pretraining learns general representations from unlabeled data, it completely eliminates the need for data selection or quality filtering during the pretraining stage.", "explanation": "False. Self-supervised pretraining at web scale remains highly sensitive to pretraining data quality; uncurated corpora containing boilerplate, corrupted text, toxic samples, or pervasive duplicates degrade representation quality and inflate training FLOPs without improving downstream transfer.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the misconception that self-supervised learning eliminates data curation requirements."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: What is the systems-level 'homogenization risk' (or blast radius) of pretraining a shared foundation model on an uncurated dataset?", "md": "A shared base model can propagate the same upstream defect into many downstream tasks, so a bad pretraining-data choice has a much larger blast radius than in train-from-scratch workflows. For example, a memorized private pattern or systematic bias in the base model may affect many adaptations built on top of it. The actual impact is not universal, because downstream fine-tuning and validation can reduce or expose some defects."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain confirmation bias in pseudo-labeling and evaluate what confidence thresholding can and cannot fix.", "You can now evaluate the task-dependent assumption behind consistency regularization.", "You can now calculate the break-even threshold for amortizing self-supervised pretraining across downstream tasks.", "You can now compare contrastive self-supervised learning architectures and their memory-compute scaling trade-offs."]}], "id": "v1-09-06-learning-from-unlabeled-data", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-09-07-selection-engineering": {"title": "Selection Engineering", "summary": "Analyze the hardware empathy principles governing storage I/O and shuffle buffer design.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["Selection Engineering", "The selection bottleneck", "The random-access penalty", "Data echoing", "Selection across multiple accelerators"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Selection Engineering** in *Data Selection* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze the hardware empathy principles governing storage I/O and shuffle buffer design\n- Compare upstream and downstream echoing by how much pipeline work they amortize and how much sample diversity they preserve\n- Evaluate pipeline balance conditions under which data echoing provides zero throughput improvement\n- Compare centralized, shard-local, and hierarchical architectures for distributed coreset selection\n- Analyze score staleness in distributed active learning and choose a reproducible refresh strategy based on measured rank agreement"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, § Selection Engineering", "md": "A naive active learning loop that scans the entire dataset every epoch to select the \"best\" samples can turn a compute-bound training job into an I/O-bound bottleneck. **Selection engineering** is the work of making a chosen technique deliver its promised speedup on real hardware and real data pipelines. The pipeline must keep scoring cheap, reads sequential, and the accelerator fed."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- The selection bottleneck\n- The random-access penalty\n- Data echoing\n- Selection across multiple accelerators\n\nPay attention to: Random-access penalty and sharded loading; Data echoing; Centralized versus hierarchical selection across workers; Stale scores in multi-worker active learning."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Contrast upstream data echoing (echoing before data augmentation) with downstream data echoing (echoing after augmentation) in terms of computational overhead and sample diversity.", "md": "Upstream echoing amortizes work before augmentation and lets repeated passes receive fresh random augmentations, so it can preserve more view diversity while still reducing some repeated upstream overhead. Downstream echoing amortizes earlier work through augmentation, but repeats the same already-augmented tensors rather than creating new views. The practical implication is to place the echo point after the bottleneck stage whose work you want to amortize, then check whether the repeated data still preserves target quality."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-07-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Why do naive random sample lookups across non-contiguous indices in large un-sharded dataset files severely degrade I/O throughput on storage hardware, and how do shuffle buffers mitigate this?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Storage devices (HDDs, SATA SSDs, NVMe SSDs, and cloud object stores) provide peak throughput under large, contiguous sequential reads. Non-contiguous random 4 KB reads cut realized bandwidth (about 375$\\times$ on HDDs, about 14$\\times$ on SATA SSDs, and under 2$\\times$ on NVMe SSDs). Shuffle buffers co-design data loading by reading large sequential shards into host RAM and performing pseudo-random shuffling locally within memory, preserving peak sequential disk bandwidth. The other choices describe fictitious hardware or software failures.", "type": "single", "options": ["Random lookups bypass host CPU caches, forcing floating-point registers to re-encode all labels", "Random lookups violate PCIe parity checks, causing GPU kernel timeouts during backward passes", "Random lookups trigger hash collisions in the Python garbage collector, halting dataloading threads", "Random 4 KB reads fall below peak sequential storage bandwidth due to IOPS limits, by hundreds of times on HDDs, whereas shuffle buffers read large sequential chunks and randomize locally in memory"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the hardware empathy principles governing storage I/O and shuffle buffer design."]}, {"q": "If the GPU training step takes 20 ms and the CPU data loading/augmentation pipeline takes 10 ms ($R = T_{\\text{pipeline}} / T_{\\text{GPU}} = 0.5$), applying a data echoing factor of $e = 2$ will double the end-to-end training throughput.", "explanation": "False. When $R < 1$, the training job is accelerator-bound (the data pipeline is already faster than the GPU), so the GPU is never starved for data. Applying data echoing in this regime provides zero throughput gain and merely feeds duplicate or stale data to the accelerator.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate pipeline balance conditions under which data echoing provides zero throughput improvement."]}, {"q": "On one host with two to eight accelerator workers, how can centralized coreset selection become a bottleneck, and what trade-off does hierarchical selection introduce?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Centralized selection gathers candidate scores or embeddings at a coordinator process on the same host. Its memory demand and CPU dispatch can compete with decoding and batch preparation, so adding accelerator workers does not necessarily accelerate selection. Hierarchical selection distributes selection by having workers select local coresets on their shards before merging at the coordinator; however, this shard-local pruning can introduce distribution skew if rare classes or boundary cases are unevenly distributed across shards. The other choices state incorrect hardware, communication, or backpropagation limitations.", "type": "single", "options": ["Centralized selection requires all workers to share a single GPU; hierarchical selection distributes weights across SSDs", "Centralized selection fails because sharding prevents network cards from transmitting floating-point values", "Centralized selection can bottleneck host memory and coordinator dispatch; hierarchical selection prunes locally per shard, risking loss of globally rare samples across shards", "Centralized selection eliminates gradient synchronization; hierarchical selection disables local backpropagation"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: compare centralized, shard-local, and hierarchical architectures for distributed coreset selection."]}, {"q": "In distributed active learning, Worker A scores candidate samples using checkpoint step $t$, while Worker B has already advanced the shared model to step $t+100$. What consistency challenge arises, and what is the best response?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The issue is ranking staleness: uncertainty scores from an older checkpoint can differ from those produced by a newer model. The chapter's remedy is not to assume such rankings are automatically invalid, but to make them reproducible with checkpoint versioning and to set refresh frequency from measured agreement decay. The other choices confuse the problem with autograd deadlock, storage overwrites, or memory exhaustion.", "type": "single", "options": ["Worker A encounters deadlock in CUDA streams; disable autograd during scoring", "Worker B overwrites Worker A's local storage; mount the dataset read-only", "Worker A's GPU runs out of memory; reduce batch size to 1", "Worker A may produce stale rankings that differ from fresher ones; version checkpoints and choose the score-refresh interval from measured ranking agreement"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: analyze score staleness in distributed active learning and choose a reproducible refresh strategy based on measured rank agreement."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Explain why performing independent, shard-local coreset pruning on an unstratified, partitioned dataset can cause minority class collapse during distributed training.", "md": "When a dataset is sharded across workers without stratification, rare minority classes may appear in very few shards. If each worker independently selects its top-$k$ coreset based on local metrics, workers with only a few minority samples may discard them as outliers, systematically erasing the minority class from the global training corpus."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze the hardware empathy principles governing storage I/O and shuffle buffer design.", "You can now compare upstream and downstream echoing by how much pipeline work they amortize and how much sample diversity they preserve.", "You can now evaluate pipeline balance conditions under which data echoing provides zero throughput improvement.", "You can now compare centralized, shard-local, and hierarchical architectures for distributed coreset selection."]}], "id": "v1-09-07-selection-engineering", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-09-08-choosing-a-selection-strategy": {"title": "Choosing a Selection Strategy", "summary": "Calculate break-even run count and ROI for a one-time static-selection investment.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["Choosing a Selection Strategy", "Data costs and return on investment", "Break-even analysis", "Amortization across training runs", "Decision process"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Choosing a Selection Strategy** in *Data Selection* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate break-even run count and ROI for a one-time static-selection investment\n- Analyze when an upstream filtering cost is justified under the chapter's four-term total-cost model\n- Evaluate non-compute engineering overheads in the data selection ROI equation\n- Apply the data selection decision framework to match operational constraints with appropriate selection paradigms\n- Compare static and dynamic selection choices using amortization, reuse, and measured cost-quality trade-offs"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, § Choosing a Selection Strategy", "md": "The hospital team in notebook \\ref{nbk-data-selection-active-learning-roi} could not label every scan, because doing so would have cost …, … over its budget. Allocating capital between human annotation and accelerator compute is a primary systems decision, and a team under a fixed budget must determine whether the next dollar buys more downstream accuracy as specialist annotations or as additional accelerator hours. A cost model settles the question."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Data costs and return on investment\n- Break-even analysis\n- Amortization across training runs\n- Decision process\n\nPay attention to: Coreset return on investment; Total cost of data; Engineering costs in ROI; Constraint-driven technique choice."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why calculating Return on Investment (ROI) for data selection requires tracking engineering implementation and pipeline maintenance costs in addition to raw accelerator compute hours.", "md": "A data selection method that saves GPU training hours may require custom storage infrastructure, indexing services, proxy model scoring pipelines, and human maintenance. If these engineering overheads exceed the cloud compute savings, the net systems ROI is negative despite apparent model FLOP reductions."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-08-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A company invests $\\$30{,}000$ to compute a high-quality coreset. Training on the full dataset costs $\\$10{,}000$ per run, whereas training on the coreset costs $\\$4{,}000$ per run. What is the break-even number of training runs, and what is the ROI after 10 runs?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Each run saves $\\$10{,}000-\\$4{,}000=\\$6{,}000$, so break-even is $\\$30{,}000/\\$6{,}000=5$ runs. After 10 runs, gross savings are \\$10\\times\\$6{,}000=\\$60{,}000$, net savings are $\\$60{,}000-\\$30{,}000=\\$30{,}000$, and ROI is $\\$30{,}000/\\$30{,}000=1.0$, or \\$100\\%$. The other answers use the wrong per-run savings or the wrong ROI arithmetic.", "type": "single", "options": ["Break-even is 5 runs, and ROI after 10 runs is \\$100\\%", "Break-even is 3 runs, and ROI after 10 runs is \\$300\\%", "Break-even is 8 runs, and ROI after 10 runs is \\$50\\%", "Break-even is 10 runs, and ROI after 10 runs is \\$0\\%"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: calculate break-even run count and ROI for a one-time static-selection investment."]}, {"q": "Using the chapter's cost model $C_{\\text{total}} = C_{\\text{acquire}} + C_{\\text{label}} + C_{\\text{store}} + C_{\\text{process}}$, which scenario best justifies an upstream filtering investment?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The chapter's four-term cost model says filtering earns its place only when the costs it avoids downstream exceed the filtering investment at matched quality. That means measuring reductions in later labeling, storage, or processing costs rather than assuming that filtering a large fraction of samples automatically saves money. Sending everything downstream ignores preventable cost, the full-manual-review claim is wrong because it asserts filtering is always worse than labeling without the measurements needed to support that universal comparison, and eliminating evaluation removes the evidence needed to claim target-quality preservation.", "type": "single", "options": ["Skip filtering so that maximum raw volume reaches training, regardless of downstream processing and labeling cost", "Spend $\\$5{,}000$ on automated filtering only if measured avoided labeling, storage, and processing costs exceed that amount while task coverage and target quality still hold", "Double human labeling effort to manually review every raw sample before filtering, because filtering cost is always worse than labeling cost", "Cut evaluation entirely to offset the cost of training on unfiltered data"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: analyze when an upstream filtering cost is justified under the chapter's four-term total-cost model."]}, {"q": "According to the chapter's decision framework, if an ML team has an abundant pool of unlabeled domain data, very limited annotation budget, and access to human domain experts for selective queries, which technique branch is recommended?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. When the primary bottleneck is labeling cost, the decision framework routes based on oracle availability: if a human oracle is available, active learning queries the most informative samples; when combined with unlabeled data, semi-supervised learning leverages structural priors. Pure augmentation does not provide ground truth for novel concepts, generative self-instruct cannot replace domain experts where ground-truth verification is required, and pairwise deduplication is a pruning step rather than a labeling strategy.", "type": "single", "options": ["Pure transformation-based data augmentation without labeling", "Active learning (human-in-the-loop selective query) or semi-supervised learning", "Generative self-instruct synthesis to replace all human annotators entirely", "Exhaustive pairwise Jaccard deduplication across all unlabeled samples"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: apply the data selection decision framework to match operational constraints with appropriate selection paradigms."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: When deciding between static coreset pruning and dynamic online active selection for a production pipeline, what role does the expected number of training runs ($N$) play in the architectural choice?", "md": "The expected run count determines how much upfront static-selection cost can be amortized. If the same curated subset will support many retrains, sweeps, or ablations, a static investment can pay off quickly; if there is only one run, that same cost may still be worth it, but only if its measured quality and savings beat the one-run baseline. Dynamic selection is not automatically cheaper, because it can add repeated scoring cost during training instead of paying once upfront."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: In recommendation systems like DLRM where embedding tables consume terabytes of memory, how does interaction deduplication differ in systems impact from cold embedding pruning?", "md": "Interaction deduplication removes repeated user-item examples, so under a fixed epoch count and fixed per-sample work it means fewer examples are actually processed, reducing total traffic and total training work. Cold-ID pruning or reindexing reduces allocated embedding capacity only when IDs disappear from the table design, but that alone does not guarantee lower realized memory traffic or bandwidth cost. A useful deployment check is whether removing rare IDs hurts recommendation quality on sparse users or items."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate break-even run count and ROI for a one-time static-selection investment.", "You can now analyze when an upstream filtering cost is justified under the chapter's four-term total-cost model.", "You can now evaluate non-compute engineering overheads in the data selection ROI equation.", "You can now apply the data selection decision framework to match operational constraints with appropriate selection paradigms."]}], "id": "v1-09-08-choosing-a-selection-strategy", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-09-09-cross-layer-interactions": {"title": "Cross-Layer Interactions", "summary": "Calculate ideal multiplicative speedup across data, algorithm, and machine levers under the chapter's fixed-assumption scenario.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["Cross-Layer Interactions"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Cross-Layer Interactions** in *Data Selection* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate ideal multiplicative speedup across data, algorithm, and machine levers under the chapter's fixed-assumption scenario\n- Compare how workload reduction and model compression target different cost terms in the optimization stack\n- Distinguish the training-work reduction that data selection delivers from the deployment savings that require model compression and measurement\n- Explain how representational deficits introduced by data selection propagate into compression and deployment costs"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, § Cross-Layer Interactions", "md": "Halving a training corpus halves the forward and backward passes, yet the resulting model may be no cheaper to compress or serve. Real ML systems combine data selection with model compression and hardware acceleration, and these cross-layer interactions amplify or undermine each other in ways no single layer reveals."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: Multiplicative compounding in the Iron Law; Data selection versus model compression; Training savings versus deployment savings; Downstream inheritance of upstream deficits."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A team curates its training corpus to half its original size and reports that the resulting model will be cheaper to deploy. What does the reduction actually change, and what evidence would support the deployment claim?", "md": "Halving the corpus halves the forward and backward passes of training, but the trained model keeps the same architecture and parameter count, so its serving cost is unchanged by the reduction itself. A curated corpus may yield more compact representations that later compression can exploit, but that is a hypothesis to verify, not a property of every selected dataset. Supporting the deployment claim requires compressing the model and measuring serving latency and task accuracy on the target hardware, rather than inferring deployment savings from the upstream dataset reduction."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-09-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In an idealized compute-bound training scenario with fixed target quality and negligible new overheads, a team halves the number of processed examples, halves the operations per processed example on the same compute-critical path, and doubles realized arithmetic throughput. What total speedup follows from the chapter's multiplicative D·A·M reasoning?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Under the chapter's ideal multiplicative setup, halving examples gives a \\$2\\times$ workload reduction, halving compute on the same path gives another \\$2\\times$, and doubling realized throughput gives a final \\$2\\times$, for \\$2\\times2\\times2=8\\times$. This reasoning assumes fixed target quality, fixed effective batch/schedule assumptions, negligible new overheads, and that the saved work lies on the compute-critical path. Measured end-to-end speedup can be smaller once other bottlenecks appear.", "type": "single", "options": ["An \\$8× speedup, because halving workload, halving per-sample work, and doubling throughput multiply on the same critical path", "A \\$6× speedup, because the factors add across layers", "A \\$2× speedup, because only the hardware improvement matters once arithmetic throughput doubles", "A \\$4× speedup, because reducing training examples and reducing per-sample work are the same effect"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: calculate ideal multiplicative speedup across data, algorithm, and machine levers under the chapter's fixed-assumption scenario."]}, {"q": "Why are upstream data selection and downstream model compression complementary rather than interchangeable?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The chapter separates two different levers: data selection changes how much workload is processed during training, while model compression changes properties of the trained model artifact and can reduce execution cost per example when the deployment hardware and software stack can exploit it. Post-training compression can help serving, but it does not retroactively remove past training work. The two are therefore complementary rather than interchangeable, and their gains are not guaranteed to multiply cleanly in every pipeline.", "type": "single", "options": ["Model compression can only be applied to computer vision models, whereas data selection is restricted to NLP", "Data selection eliminates backward passes entirely, whereas model compression eliminates forward passes", "Data selection optimizes only inference latency on edge devices, whereas model compression only affects training time", "Data selection reduces how many examples are processed, whereas model compression reduces the artifact size and can lower execution cost per example on compatible hardware"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: compare how workload reduction and model compression target different cost terms in the optimization stack."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Explain how an aggressive data filter that discards rare but critical edge cases can raise costs in the compression and deployment stages of the optimization stack.", "md": "Each downstream stage inherits both the computational savings and any representational deficits created upstream. If the filter removes rare edge cases, the trained model may already sit close to its accuracy threshold on those cases, leaving little headroom for compression. Engineers may then have to train longer or abandon aggressive quantization to preserve task accuracy, which gives back part of the saving. The practical implication is that a selection policy must be evaluated on the deployed model's accuracy and latency, not only on training FLOPs saved."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate ideal multiplicative speedup across data, algorithm, and machine levers under the chapter's fixed-assumption scenario.", "You can now compare how workload reduction and model compression target different cost terms in the optimization stack.", "You can now distinguish the training-work reduction that data selection delivers from the deployment savings that require model compression and measurement.", "You can now explain how representational deficits introduced by data selection propagate into compression and deployment costs."]}], "id": "v1-09-09-cross-layer-interactions", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-09-10-fallacies-and-pitfalls": {"title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls", "summary": "Analyze why benchmark data pruning ratios fail to transfer to specialized production domains.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-09", "sections": ["Fallacies and Pitfalls", "Summary", "Summary"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Fallacies and Pitfalls** in *Data Selection* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze why benchmark data pruning ratios fail to transfer to specialized production domains\n- Analyze violations of the Selection Inequality in complex data selection architectures\n- Evaluate the pitfall of aggregate metric optimization under severe class imbalance\n- Evaluate the misconception that high synthetic fidelity rules out recursive-training failure modes"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Data Selection, § Fallacies and Pitfalls", "md": "Scaling a training corpus tenfold can multiply storage, I/O, and accelerator bills while barely moving test accuracy. Yet an uncalibrated selection filter can just as easily spend more time scoring candidates than it saves in training or silently erase a safety-critical rare class. The entries below range from mistaken beliefs about what data selection can achieve to failures that arise when a correct strategy meets a systems bottleneck or when a benchmark result is applied uncritically to a new domain."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Summary\n\nPay attention to: Benchmark pruning ratio transfer fallacy; Selection inequality overhead pitfall; Aggregate accuracy vs minority class erasure; Recursive synthetic training collapse."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why is evaluating a data selection strategy solely on aggregate validation accuracy a dangerous pitfall when dealing with imbalanced datasets?", "md": "In imbalanced datasets, aggregate accuracy is heavily dominated by majority classes. A data pruning algorithm could discard \\$90\\% of rare minority or safety-critical examples to optimize overall compute while aggregate accuracy appears unchanged, completely compromising model performance on rare real-world failure modes."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-10-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A research paper reports that an EL2N coreset selection method successfully pruned \\$50\\%$ of CIFAR-10 with \\$0\\%$ loss in accuracy. A medical imaging team applies the exact same \\$50\\%$ pruning ratio to a rare tumor detection dataset and suffers a disastrous \\$28\\%$ drop in recall. What fallacy explains this failure?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Pruning ratios calibrated on balanced, homogeneous academic benchmarks like CIFAR-10 rarely transfer to specialized production domains like medical imaging. In domains with extreme class imbalance or subtle pathological features, a \\$50\\% global prune disproportionately discards rare minority samples and critical edge cases, causing severe domain-specific performance drops. Hardware precision and class counts are irrelevant to this transfer fallacy.", "type": "single", "options": ["The team failed to use GPU acceleration during the inference pass", "The team used float32 precision instead of bfloat16 mixed precision", "CIFAR-10 contains more total classes than medical imaging datasets", "Assuming that benchmark coreset pruning ratios transfer directly to specialized, highly imbalanced production domains with rare failure modes"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: analyze why benchmark data pruning ratios fail to transfer to specialized production domains."]}, {"q": "A team implements an elaborate multi-stage active learning pipeline that reduces training dataset size by \\$40\\%. However, the continuous clustering, proxy scoring, and cross-worker all-gather synchronization take 3 times longer than the GPU time saved during training. Which pitfall does this represent?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. This is the canonical violation of the Selection Inequality: an engineering team designs a selection algorithm that successfully reduces sample count, but the computational and synchronization overhead of scoring and filtering ($T_{\\text{selection}}$) exceeds the training time saved on the reduced subset, leading to a net increase in total wall-clock time. Model collapse relates to recursive generative training, NVMe alignment relates to I/O access patterns, and the smoothness assumption belongs to semi-supervised learning.", "type": "single", "options": ["Violating the Selection Inequality by incurring selection overheads that exceed downstream training savings ($T_{\\text{selection}} > \\Delta T_{\\text{train}}$)", "Encountering model collapse due to recursive generator loops", "Failing to implement 4 KB small-read alignment on host NVMe storage", "Violating the smoothness assumption in semi-supervised consistency regularization"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze violations of the Selection Inequality in complex data selection architectures."]}, {"q": "Because modern high-capacity generative models produce photorealistic images and fluent text, a model trained on recursively generated synthetic data will never suffer performance degradation.", "explanation": "False. The chapter warns that recursive synthetic training can cause model collapse by progressively eroding rare patterns and compounding earlier errors. High generator quality can reduce the risk in some settings, but it does not make degradation impossible.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the misconception that high synthetic fidelity rules out recursive-training failure modes."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze why benchmark data pruning ratios fail to transfer to specialized production domains.", "You can now analyze violations of the Selection Inequality in complex data selection architectures.", "You can now evaluate the pitfall of aggregate metric optimization under severe class imbalance.", "You can now evaluate the misconception that high synthetic fidelity rules out recursive-training failure modes."]}], "id": "v1-09-10-fallacies-and-pitfalls", "chapter": "v1-09", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-09-99-test": {"title": "Test: Data Selection", "kind": "test", "summary": "Check what you learned across the whole chapter.", "blocks": [{"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-09-test", "pass_score": 0.7, "questions": [{"q": "A team distills a ResNet-50 student on ImageNet and chooses offline distillation, precomputing the teacher's FP32 logits for every training image instead of keeping the teacher in accelerator memory. Which cost does this choice move onto the data path?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Offline distillation stores one logit per class for every sample, so the per-sample label grows from a single integer index to four bytes per class, an expansion of more than three orders of magnitude for ImageNet's thousand classes, and every epoch reads it back through the input pipeline. Teacher residency and the reduced student batch size are the costs of online distillation, which this choice avoids. Soft targets do not force a lower learning rate, and the teacher pass runs once during precomputation rather than once per epoch.", "type": "single", "options": ["Every epoch must read per-class logit vectors in place of single integer labels, multiplying the label bytes the storage tier and input pipeline must deliver", "Every training step must run a teacher forward pass beside the student, restricting the student's per-accelerator batch size", "The student must be trained with a lower learning rate, because soft targets produce larger gradients than one-hot labels", "The teacher must be retrained once per student epoch, so preparation cost grows linearly with the number of epochs"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze knowledge distillation as a data-synthesis lever by locating its storage and input-pipeline cost."]}, {"q": "Order the following stages as they are presented in this chapter's conceptual map of the data-selection pipeline:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (2) Static Pruning, (1) Dynamic Selection, (3) Synthetic Data Generation. This is the chapter's presentation order: prune low-value data before target training, adapt sample usage during training, then create missing coverage when needed. The sequence is a conceptual map rather than a mandatory runtime chronology, because synthetic data can also be created earlier and then pruned or selected before final training.", "type": "order", "items": ["Static Pruning", "Dynamic Selection", "Synthetic Data Generation."], "hints": ["Think about: identify the chapter's conceptual ordering of the three main data-selection intervention points."]}, {"q": "In curriculum learning, an engineer implements an easy-to-hard pacing schedule that controls the fraction of the sorted training pool available at each stage. What is the primary objective of this pacing strategy?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The chapter presents curriculum learning as a way to shape the optimization trajectory: easier examples can provide steadier early gradients, while harder examples are introduced later as the model matures. That is not a guarantee against divergence on every task, and 'easy' depends on the scoring rule, but it is the pacing mechanism this section teaches. The other choices confuse curriculum with memory-layout tricks or active-learning workflows.", "type": "single", "options": ["To guide early optimization with more stable examples before gradually introducing harder ones", "To eliminate the need for backward passes during the first half of training", "To maximize GPU memory bandwidth utilization by sorting tensors strictly by length in bytes", "To replace human labelers with an automated oracle during the late stages of training"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze how curriculum pacing can stabilize early learning by controlling when harder examples appear."]}, {"q": "Order the steps in an active learning closed-loop iteration:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (3) Score unlabeled pool using current model, (1) Select top query samples via query strategy, (2) Acquire expert annotations from the oracle, (5) Add newly labeled samples to the training set, (4) Retrain or update model on expanded dataset. The cycle begins with proxy or model scoring over the candidate pool, selecting candidate queries, querying the human oracle, incorporating verified labels into the dataset, and updating the model.", "type": "order", "items": ["Score unlabeled pool using current model", "Select top query samples via query strategy", "Acquire expert annotations from the oracle", "Add newly labeled samples to the training set.", "Retrain or update model on expanded dataset"], "hints": ["Think about: design the closed-loop workflow of an iterative active learning system."]}, {"q": "Because self-supervised pretraining learns general representations from unlabeled data, it completely eliminates the need for data selection or quality filtering during the pretraining stage.", "explanation": "False. Self-supervised pretraining at web scale remains highly sensitive to pretraining data quality; uncurated corpora containing boilerplate, corrupted text, toxic samples, or pervasive duplicates degrade representation quality and inflate training FLOPs without improving downstream transfer.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the misconception that self-supervised learning eliminates data curation requirements."]}, {"q": "On one host with two to eight accelerator workers, how can centralized coreset selection become a bottleneck, and what trade-off does hierarchical selection introduce?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Centralized selection gathers candidate scores or embeddings at a coordinator process on the same host. Its memory demand and CPU dispatch can compete with decoding and batch preparation, so adding accelerator workers does not necessarily accelerate selection. Hierarchical selection distributes selection by having workers select local coresets on their shards before merging at the coordinator; however, this shard-local pruning can introduce distribution skew if rare classes or boundary cases are unevenly distributed across shards. The other choices state incorrect hardware, communication, or backpropagation limitations.", "type": "single", "options": ["Centralized selection requires all workers to share a single GPU; hierarchical selection distributes weights across SSDs", "Centralized selection fails because sharding prevents network cards from transmitting floating-point values", "Centralized selection can bottleneck host memory and coordinator dispatch; hierarchical selection prunes locally per shard, risking loss of globally rare samples across shards", "Centralized selection eliminates gradient synchronization; hierarchical selection disables local backpropagation"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: compare centralized, shard-local, and hierarchical architectures for distributed coreset selection."]}, {"q": "Under the illustrative analytical model where dataset information content scales as $\\mathcal{I}(D) \\propto \\log D$ and training compute scales linearly as $O = O_{\\text{sample}} \\cdot D$, how does the marginal Information-Compute Ratio $\\mathrm{ICR}(D)$ scale with dataset size $D$?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Under the chapter's illustrative model, $d\\mathcal{I}/dD \\propto 1/D$ and $dO/dD = O_{\\text{sample}}$, so their ratio is $\\mathrm{ICR}(D) \\approx 1/(O_{\\text{sample}}D)$. This captures diminishing marginal learning signal under the stated log-information and linear-compute assumptions. A constant or growing answer ignores the derivative of $\\log D$, while the exponential-decay choice introduces a memory-capacity mechanism that is not part of this model.", "type": "single", "options": ["It remains constant at $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ because additional compute scales proportionally with dataset size", "It decays as $\\mathcal{O}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{O_{\\text{sample}}D}\\right)$, so under this illustrative model additional data eventually adds compute faster than marginal information", "It grows logarithmically as $\\mathcal{O}(\\log D / O_{\\text{sample}})$ due to power-law parameter scaling", "It decays exponentially as $\\mathcal{O}(\\exp(-D))$ once the training corpus exceeds accelerator memory capacity"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the scaling behavior of the Information-Compute Ratio under the section's illustrative logarithmic-information model."]}, {"q": "Using a high-capacity diffusion model or state-of-the-art LLM to generate synthetic training data guarantees that the resulting training set will be completely free of distribution shift relative to the real deployment environment.", "explanation": "False. Generator quality does not eliminate domain gap. Synthetic generators can amplify subtle distribution shifts, introduce generator-specific artifacts, and omit edge cases present in real-world deployment environments, requiring domain validation against real ground truth.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the misconception that advanced generative models eliminate domain shift."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Compute-optimal frontier**?", "options": ["Sample value is learning per unit of training compute (the information-compute ratio), learning-curve metrics show whether a reduction held quality, and a gain in data value becomes a throughput gain only when the run is compute-bound and the saved work is on the critical path.", "Controlled sweeps fit a compute-optimal balance of about 20 tokens per parameter, so a run whose unique tokens per parameter sit well below it is data-starved and repeats its corpus, which is the regime where selection rather than more compute moves the run toward the frontier.", "Semi-supervised and self-supervised learning trade extra training compute for fewer task labels, valid only when the unlabeled pool matches the target distribution, and a foundation model pays off only when pretraining is amortized across enough downstream tasks.", "When the pool lacks the needed cases, augmentation and generative synthesis replace curation at a per-sample cost against realism, the domain gap needs randomization, adaptation, and a real-data anchor, and recursive training risks model collapse."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Compute-optimal frontier:** Controlled sweeps fit a compute-optimal balance of about 20 tokens per parameter, so a run whose unique tokens per parameter sit well below it is data-starved and repeats its corpus, which is the regime where selection rather than more compute moves the run toward the frontier.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Compute-optimal frontier itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"q": "In an ML infrastructure scaling scenario where available GPU compute grows by approximately \\$10\\times$ every 3 years while high-quality web data grows by only \\$2\\times$ every 5 years, what primary systems regime emerges, and what is the appropriate systems response?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Compute throughput growing faster than accessible high-quality human data creates a compute-to-data imbalance known as the data wall. In this regime, additional raw compute yields diminishing returns on uncurated corpora, making data selection essential to maximize the Information-Compute Ratio (ICR). Shifting model parallelism addresses parameter memory limits rather than data scarcity, scaling disk bandwidth solves I/O stalls rather than token information quality, and eliminating synthetic generation ignores a key strategy for expanding scarce data pools.", "type": "single", "options": ["A compute-rich, data-constrained data wall regime where intelligent data selection and curation must maximize the learning signal extracted per token", "A memory bandwidth-bound regime where model parallel sharding must replace data parallelism across all training clusters", "An I/O ingestion bottleneck where disk read bandwidth must be quadrupled to keep GPUs saturated", "A compute-starved regime where synthetic data generation should be eliminated to avoid wasting accelerator cycles"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the systems implications of the compute-to-data scaling asymmetry and diagnose the data wall regime."]}, {"q": "The training-dynamics coreset metric that measures the Euclidean norm of the difference between predicted class probabilities and the one-hot target vector early in training is known as ____.", "explanation": "EL2N. It measures $\\lVert p(x)-y \\rVert_2$ early in training as an error-based difficulty score. The chapter notes that proxy-model rankings can transfer usefully to larger targets, but that transfer must be validated rather than assumed universal.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["EL2N"], "hints": ["Think about: identify EL2N from its mathematical definition and explain why proxy-based use still requires validation."]}], "mode": "test"}], "id": "v1-09-99-test", "chapter": "v1-09", "minutes": 10}});
